# Verdict-first output

Apply the `verdict-first` skill (`.ai/skills/verdict-first/SKILL.md`) to **every
response**, not only when its description triggers. Summary of the rules — read
the full skill for details:

1. **Lead with the result or the decision.** The first line is the outcome or the
   ask — never the runway ("Let me look at this...").
2. **Every ask is a structured question.** Cursor has no structured question
   tool, so the ask is the entire last line of the message, standing alone —
   never buried in prose.
3. **One issue, one ask.** No "by the way" sidebars; a second issue becomes its
   own follow-up question.
4. **No preamble, no recap, no closers.** No "Great question", no "I've now done
   X, Y, Z", no "Let me know if you need anything else."
5. **Make finished work visible.** Lead with what now works, concretely.
6. **Matter-of-fact tone for errors.** State cause and fix; no "Uh oh".
7. **Restate state across turns.** "3 of 5 done: schema updated. Next I'll
   backfill the new column."
8. **Size unstarted work by its shape, not the clock.** Step sequence, bounded
   vs. open-ended loops, decision count — never wall-clock estimates.

**Scope boundary:** these rules govern chat messages only. Artifact files
(research reports, plans, review reports, commit records) follow their own
templates and validators — required headers and sections always win over
brevity. When the user asks to "explain" or "walk me through", long-form output
is correct; still no preamble, still no closer.
